VERIFIED WORKING:
  (1) Task action quoting is correct — schtasks parsed '"python.exe" "runner.py"' into separate <Command>/<Arguments> XML elements; verified via schtasks /query /tn StockIngestion-DailyLoad /xml. Task actually ran 2026-09-12 15:11, Last Result 0; log shows NYSE-closed skip + CHECKPOINT + clean detach.
  (2) daily_load_once.py is cwd-independent (sys.path bootstrap from __file__, absolute config path) — more robust than scheduler_main.py's relative 'src/config.yaml'. Same code path as daemon: PipelineScheduler.run_once -> _run_with_retry (enabled gate, DB lock retry w/ backoff, NYSE calendar in cfg.scheduler.timezone, run_daily_load(lookback_days=1), optional R2 sync). Consistent with scheduler/CI=1 convention.
  (3) Idempotent re-registration via /f; -Unregister works (schtasks /delete /tn /f); missing runner -> Write-Error terminating (EA=Stop), nonzero exit. MultipleInstancesPolicy=IgnoreNew prevents overlapping runs.
  (4) Mandatory checks: check_loops + --strict exit 0 (14 files); pytest 235 passed exit 0.

ISSUES:
  I1 (HIGH — correctness on this machine): TIMEZONE MISMATCH. schtasks /st schedules in LOCAL machine time; the .ps1 reads schedule_time from config.yaml but ignores scheduler.timezone. Machine TZ is 'GMT Standard Time' (UK); config scheduler.timezone is 'America/New_York'. Live task fires 16:14 UK = 11:14 ET — MID-SESSION, ~5h before NYSE close. Daily load will upsert a partial intraday bar for the current day (self-heals next run via lookback, but 'daily close' semantics violated same-day). ET/UK offset also drifts 1h during DST-gap weeks. Fix: convert schedule_time from cfg.scheduler.timezone to local time in the .ps1, or generate task XML with timezone-aware StartBoundary.
  I2 (HIGH — silent failure, VERIFIED LIVE): DB-lock failure exits 0. Ran daily_load_once.py while duckdb.exe (PID 17532) held C:\Intel\parquet\stock_ingestion.duckdb: 4 retries over ~7.5s, ERROR logged, process exited 0. Task Scheduler 'Last Result' cannot distinguish lock-failure from success. If the Flight daemon (src/main.py) holds a persistent RW conn, every scheduled run silently no-ops. Fix: run_once should surface failure status; script should sys.exit(nonzero) when the load did not execute.
  I3 (MEDIUM — diagnosability): exceptions in _execute_daily_load propagate uncaught through run_once -> traceback to stderr -> discarded (no console for scheduled tasks). Log file gets nothing; only Last Result=1. Fix: wrap run_once in try/except + logging.exception + sys.exit(1) in daily_load_once.py.
  I4 (LOW — drift/evidence): registered task StartBoundary is 15:11, not 16:14 as executor evidence claims (registered 2026-09-12T15:10:49, likely -Time override for immediate testing). Config.yaml schedule_time changes do NOT propagate to the registered task — re-run the .ps1 to re-sync. Current live task is stale vs config.
  I5 (LOW — deployment edge cases): (a) Get-Command python captures first-on-PATH interpreter at registration time — Store-alias stub or a venv-less python would break the task (here resolved correctly to C:\Intel\python\3.12\python.exe); consider -PythonExe param or dependency probe. (b) LogonType=InteractiveToken — no run when user logged off; DisallowStartIfOnBatteries/StopIfGoingOnBatteries defaults skip on laptop battery (moot on this desktop). (c) schtasks strips inner quotes into <Arguments> — runner path with spaces (e.g. 'C:\Users\John Doe\...') may break argv parsing; unaffected on this machine. (d) .ps1 regex 'schedule_time:\s*[''"]?(\d{1,2}:\d{2})' takes first match anywhere in file and accepts invalid times (99:99) — schtasks /st rejects with nonzero exit, acceptable. (e) daily_load_once ignores config.logging.log_format (always text) unlike scheduler_main.py — low impact, arguably safer for the shared log file.
  PROD DB RISK: no corruption risk — transactional writes, CHECKPOINT on close, lock-open failure is a clean no-op. Real risks are missed/silent-failed loads (I2) and mid-session partial bars (I1).
  VERDICT: ISSUES FOUND — I1 and I2 should be addressed (or explicitly accepted) before feature sign-off. Both files are functional as written; issues are behavioral, not crashes.


